security remains high the day after the  election with thousands of troops  deployed across the country despite  dozens of minor attacks many afghans  expressed relief that polling day had  passed off without any major incidents  we're so happy that yesterday's election  took place in a peaceful environment  there was good security around the  country and everywhere was peaceful  people went to the polling stations with  a lot of hope despite all the threats  they took part to choose their future  leader  turnout exceeded expectations and there  were reports of ballot paper shortages  in some areas  an estimated 7 million of the 12 million  eligible voters participated in the poll  in a televised address shortly after  polling stations closed outgoing  president hamid kazai thanked people for  the high turnout and praised them for  defying terrorist threats to come out  and vote  he described the election as a step  forward and a success for the country  despite the positive mood the three  front runners have complained of  possible fraud no candidate is expected  to receive an overall majority though  many in afghanistan want to avoid a  runoff vote  we want the election finalized in the  first round because the people  government and economy are very weak a  second round would challenge the  security forces  but a runoff seems to be a foregone  conclusion and would take place at the  end of may
 

